course: technology
lesson: 3
title: Crisps, bread, cereal and dairy
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most of a supermarket's shelves are filled by machines: whole potatoes go in at one end of a crisp factory and sealed bags of crisps come out at the other.
explain: Cameras watch the crisps falling, and tiny puffs of air blow out any burnt or green one in a fraction of a second, while bread rides through a tunnel oven longer than a swimming pool.
explain: Much of food-factory work is really about germs: milk is heated to kill them, yoghurt has friendly ones added on purpose, and soup is sealed in a can before it is heated so no new germs can get in.
explain: Think of the crisp bag that seems half empty: it is full of nitrogen gas, which keeps the crisps fresh and stops them being crushed.
fact: Crisp potatoes are washed, peeled by spinning against rough rollers, sliced very thin, fried or baked, flavoured in a tumbling drum and bagged.
fact: Crisp bags are filled with nitrogen gas instead of air, which keeps crisps fresh and cushions them.
fact: Cameras watch falling crisps and puffs of air knock out dark or green ones; the same kind of sorter works on rice, nuts and peas.
fact: Green patches on potatoes go with a natural poison, which is why green crisps are removed; one small green crisp is very unlikely to harm anyone, but factories take them out to be safe.
fact: Bread is flour, water, yeast and salt mixed in huge mixers, shaped, left to rise in warm cupboards, baked, cooled, sliced and bagged.
fact: Yeast is a tiny living fungus (a relative of mushrooms), and the bubbles in bread are the gas it gives off.
fact: A tunnel oven has a moving belt: raw dough goes in at one end and comes out baked at the other, and some tunnel ovens are longer than a swimming pool.
fact: Biscuit dough is rolled into a sheet, cut by a roller with shapes, baked in a tunnel oven and wrapped; the little holes in crackers let steam out so they bake flat.
fact: Cereal flakes are cooked grains squashed flat between heavy rollers and toasted; puffed grains are heated under pressure and then released so they pop; cornflakes were invented in the 1890s at a health resort in the USA.
fact: Yoghurt is milk heated to kill germs and then kept warm for hours with friendly bacteria that thicken it; the bacteria are alive, and the fridge keeps the yoghurt from going off.
fact: Milk is cooled at the farm, carried by tanker to a dairy and heated briefly to kill germs, which is called pasteurising after Louis Pasteur, who studied germs in the 1800s.
fact: Frozen pizzas get toppings from machines, sometimes falling like snow from hoppers, then are frozen fast in a cold tunnel, because fast freezing makes small ice crystals that damage food less; they must be cooked until hot all the way through.
fact: Canned food is sealed in the can first and then the whole can is heated, so it keeps for years without a fridge; canning was invented about 200 years ago, and the first cans were opened with a hammer and chisel because the can-opener came later.
fact: Do not eat from a can that is swollen or badly dented along its seams.
exercise: How are potatoes peeled in a crisp factory? | By spinning against rough rollers | By workers with small kitchen knives | By soaking them in vinegar for a week | By blasting them with hot air
exercise: Why is a crisp bag so puffed up? | It is filled with nitrogen gas that keeps crisps fresh and cushions them | The factory adds air only to trick shoppers | The crisps slowly breathe out gas inside the bag after it has been sealed up | It is filled with steam left from frying
exercise: How does a crisp factory remove a burnt crisp? | A camera spots it and a puff of air knocks it out | A worker picks out each burnt crisp by hand as it passes | A strong magnet pulls the burnt ones off the moving belt | The burnt crisps sink to the bottom of a water tank
exercise: Why are green crisps thrown out? | Green patches on potatoes go with a natural poison | Green crisps are much too crunchy and hurt people's teeth | Nobody will ever buy a crisp that looks green in the bag | They are sold separately in other bags as mint flavour
exercise: What makes bread rise and go fluffy? | Gas bubbles given off by yeast | Air pumped into the dough through tubes | Chalk powder that fizzes in the oven | Steam from the slicing machine
exercise: What is yeast? | A tiny living fungus | A special kind of flour | A white powder made of plastic | A type of sea salt
exercise: What is a tunnel oven? | A long oven with a moving belt that bakes food on its way through | An oven built underground in a tunnel | A round oven that spins the bread | A tiny oven that bakes one loaf at a time
exercise: Why do crackers have little holes in them? | To let steam out so they bake flat | To save a little dough on each one | So they can be threaded on a string | To make them lighter to post
exercise: How are cereal flakes made? | Cooked grains are squashed flat between rollers and toasted | Grains are sliced by tiny knives | Flakes are cut very thinly from big sheets of dry toasted bread | Flakes grow ready-made on the plant
exercise: How do puffed rice grains pop up? | They are heated under pressure and then released | They are blown up one by one with a tiny air pump | They are soaked in warm water until they swell up big | They are hit with tiny hammers until they puff out
exercise: Where were cornflakes invented? | At a health resort in the USA in the 1890s | In a French bakery in the 1600s | In a space station in the 1990s | In an Irish castle in the 1700s
exercise: What makes milk into yoghurt? | Friendly bacteria kept warm in it for hours | Whisking the milk very fast | Freezing the milk and thawing it | Adding flour and boiling it
exercise: What is pasteurising? | Heating milk briefly to kill germs | Feeding cows on fresh pasture | Freezing milk for a whole year | Mixing milk with clean water
exercise: Why are frozen pizzas frozen fast in a cold tunnel? | Fast freezing makes small ice crystals that damage food less | Slow freezing is against the law for any kind of food sold in shops | Fast freezing makes the pizza grow bigger | Fast freezing melts the cheese evenly
exercise: How can canned soup keep for years? | It is sealed in the can and then heated, killing the germs inside | The can has a tiny fridge built into its lid that keeps the soup cold | Salt in the soup freezes all the germs | Tiny holes in the can let the germs escape
exercise: Which can should you never eat from? | One that is swollen or badly dented along the seams | One with a torn paper label | One that has been sitting in the cupboard for a whole month | One that has been kept in the fridge
exercise: A pack has 6 bags of crisps. Mum buys 3 packs. How many bags is that? | 18 | 9 | 12 | 63
test: «crisp bags are like 90% air, total rip-off innit» What is right? | It is nitrogen, put in to keep crisps fresh and stop them breaking | Yes, the gas is only there to cheat you | It is just air the crisps breathe out after frying, so the bag blows up | It is smoke left over from the fryer
test: «found a green crisp in me bag, will it kill me» What is the best answer? | One small crisp is very unlikely to harm you, but factories remove green ones for safety | Yes, one green crisp can be deadly | Green crisps are healthier, so eat more | Green means it is the mint flavour
test: «why's my bread got holes in, did the factory cheap out» What is right? | The holes are gas bubbles from yeast that make bread soft | Machines drill holes in each loaf to save on flour and money | Tiny worms ate through the dough | The slicing knives leave the holes
test: What would happen if a baker forgot the yeast? | The bread would come out flat and heavy | It would rise twice as high as normal bread | It would turn into a soft sponge cake instead | It would bake with no crust on it at all
test: «milk comes from a cow, why heat it, it's natural innit» What is right? | Raw milk can carry germs, so it is heated briefly to kill them | Heating is what makes milk white | Cows give milk that is already hot | Heating is done only to make the milk taste a little bit sweeter
test: «yoghurt's got bacteria in it?? minging, bacteria make u sick» What is right? | These are friendly bacteria that turn milk into yoghurt and are safe to eat | Yes, every single kind of bacteria makes you ill, so yoghurt is risky to eat | No, yoghurt has no bacteria in it at all | Only the lid of the pot has bacteria
test: «pizza's still a bit icy in the middle, whatever, eat it» What should you do? | Cook it longer until it is hot all the way through | Eat it fast before the icy middle has time to melt | Put it back in the freezer and have it another day | Eat the icy middle first while it is still nice and cold
test: A tunnel oven bakes 40 loaves a minute. How many loaves in 5 minutes? | 200 | 45 | 140 | 400
test: «tins were invented after can openers, obviously» What is right? | Tins came first and were opened with a hammer and chisel at first | Yes, can openers came 100 years earlier | They were invented on the very same day | Tins have never needed opening
test: Sara sees a can of beans with a puffed-up, swollen top. What should she do? | Not eat it and show an adult | Open it and taste a little to check | Press the top down and cook it | Shake it until the top goes flat
test: «cereal flakes just grow like that on the plant» What is right? | Cooked grains are squashed flat between heavy rollers and toasted | Yes, flakes grow on corn stalks | Flakes are cut from popcorn | Flakes are pressed from paper
test: A loaf has 20 slices. A family of 4 each eat 2 for breakfast. How many slices are left? | 12 | 16 | 8 | 18
exam: Identify the principal reason for which crisp packets are filled with nitrogen gas. | To preserve freshness and to cushion the contents against breakage | To deceive the purchaser as to the quantity of crisps contained therein | To impart flavour to the crisps | To render the packet capable of floating in air
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises pasteurisation? | The brief heating of milk in order to destroy harmful germs | The addition of water to milk | The freezing of milk for prolonged storage in cold warehouses | The removal of all fat from milk
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the rising of bread. | The dough rises owing to gas released by yeast, a living fungus | The dough rises owing to air injected into it by machines in the bakery | The dough rises owing to the heat of the slicing machine | The dough rises solely owing to the salt
exam: Ascertain the reason for which canned food may be stored for years without refrigeration. | The food is sealed within the can and subsequently heated, destroying the germs within | The can contains a concealed cooling device | The food is preserved by the paper label | Germs are unable to survive in darkness
